WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.779
He is revered as one of the greatest artists
of all time.

00:00:03.779 --> 00:00:07.780
For centuries he has typified the perfect
artistic genius.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.829
His greatest legacy is the surviving works
that we can see today - The Sistene Chapel,

00:00:12.829 --> 00:00:14.200
the Statue of david, the Pieta.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.350
Yet, behind the artworks lies a complex and
stubborn man.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:29.020
In this week’s Biographics, we go beyond
the facade to discover the real Michelangelo.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:40.590
Early Days

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:44.330
Italy in the 15th century was very different
to the country we know today.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:47.110
Back then it was a collection of small republics.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:51.970
These city states were all under the governorship
of the Pope in Rome.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:55.059
The key republics were Florence, Genoa and
Venice.

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:59.730
Every state had its own governors, nobles,
peasants, priests and government officials.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.500
Nestled in the remote Apennine Mountains of
central Italy was the small village of Caprese.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.670
It was here, on March 6th, 1475, that Francesca
di Neri Buonarroti gace birth to her second

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:11.780
son, Michelangelo.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:17.310
Her husband, Lodovico di Leonardo Buonarroti
Simoni was a lowly official who had the job

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.830
of convicting and sentencing criminals.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.869
When baby Michelangelo was sixth months old,
the family of four moved to Florence.

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:27.670
Here Lodovico bought a small farm near the
village of Settignano.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:32.510
It was in this village that the child spent
his early years, under the care of a wet nurse.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:36.159
Farming off newborns to a wet nurse for the
first two years of life was a common practice

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:37.160
at that time.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.510
The woman who took in Michelangelo was married
to a stonecutter.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:44.259
In later years, when he had transformed himself
into a sculptor, Michelangelo commented that

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:47.270
he had drunk marble dust with his nurse’s
milk.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:51.070
When he was three, Michelangelo returned to
live with his family in Florence.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:56.010
The family was poor and, with new babies arriving
frequently, the household was overcrowded.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.630
Lodovico received a meagre government salary
and his farm was not very successful.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.020
In fact, he believed that the work of a lowly
farmer was beneath him.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:08.979
When he was six years of age, young Michelangelo's
tough life got even tougher.

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:13.230
His mother died, leaving behind five young
sons, including a newborn.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.950
Lodovico did his best, working two jobs and
still managing to trundle the boys off to

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:22.050
Mass at the church of Santa Croce in Florence
every Sunday.This church was filled with magnificent

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:26.690
works of art, including a crucifix carved
by the famous sculptor Donatello and wall

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:30.390
paintings by Giotto, one of the greatest artists
of all time.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:34.540
The young Michelangelo must have marvelled
at these magnificent pieces.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.819
In 1485, Lodovico took on a new wife.

00:02:37.819 --> 00:02:43.489
Lucrezia degli Ubaldini Gagliano came from
a rich family, bringing 600 florin to the

00:02:43.489 --> 00:02:45.240
union as a dowry.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:50.160
This was an enormous amount at that time and
it flipped the fortunes of the family overnight.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.239
An immediate result of the family's newfound
wealth was that Lodovico was able to afford

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:58.940
to send Michelangelo to a Latin school to
learn reading, writing, and mathematics.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.020
But the boy soon proved that he was no academic.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:06.620
Bored with his lessons, he would stare out
the window while doodling on paper.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.570
However, he did fall in love with poetry.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:19.960
A Passion for Art

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.140
Returning home for vacation, Michelangelo
told his father that he was more interested

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.450
in art than in academic studies.

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.090
But Lodovico was not impressed.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:32.760
At that time artists were the lowly paid servant
of noblemen, being considered little more

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:33.940
than a servant.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.450
Lodovico wanted his son to aspire to more
than that.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:41.510
The only person who seemed to understand Michelangelo’s
passion for art was an older boy he had befriended

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.480
by the name of Francesco Granacci.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:49.220
Francesco was himself a budding artist, apprenticed
to the famous artist Domenico Ghirlandaio.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.269
He took Michelangelo to his master’s workshop
and showed hm works in progress.

00:03:53.269 --> 00:03:55.890
The younger boy was mesmerized by what he
saw.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.190
He knew that this was his destiny.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:04.340
Despite his father’s negativity, Michelangelo
kept begging to be allowed to pursue his passion.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:08.900
Meanwhile, his friend Francesco encouraged
him to produce some drawings, which he showed

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.110
to his master, Ghirlandaio.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:15.540
The great artist was impressed and expressed
the desire to also take Michelangelo on as

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:16.540
an apprentice.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.949
Finally Lodovico relented, and at the age
of 12, his son became apprenticed to Ghirlandaio.

00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:26.129
The master ran the most successful artists
workshop in Florence, along with his two brothers.

00:04:26.129 --> 00:04:28.559
They were kept busy decorating churches in
Florence.

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:33.009
The brothers had a number of apprentices,
aged between 8 and 15, who lived with them

00:04:33.009 --> 00:04:35.199
and assisted with paintings and murals.

00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:39.699
The parents, including Lodovico, had to pay
for living expenses and training.

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:43.610
From the Ghirlandaio brothers, young Michelangelo
learned how to prepare a wooden panel for

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:47.689
painting and how to plaster a wall in preparation
for the painting of a fresco.

00:04:47.689 --> 00:04:53.139
He was also taught how to make working drawings,
called cartoons, which would form the basis

00:04:53.139 --> 00:05:04.990
of finished paintings.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:05.990
The Prideful Apprentice

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:08.349
Among the apprentices, Michelangelo was an
immediate standout.

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:11.169
Still he proved to be stubborn, and quite
full of himself.

00:05:11.169 --> 00:05:16.629
He was given the task of copying works previously
drawn by his master and, having done so, declared

00:05:16.629 --> 00:05:21.620
that his works were better than the originals.Ghirlandaio
acknowledged that the boy had great talent,

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.509
but he was angered by his prideful attitude.

00:05:23.509 --> 00:05:28.759
He also saw that Michelangelo lacked the patience
needed to learn some of the skills required,

00:05:28.759 --> 00:05:30.300
such as painting frescoes.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.949
He showed little interest in grinding and
mixing paints and couldn't be bothered mixing

00:05:33.949 --> 00:05:38.020
the ingredients needed for the smooth plaster
needed for fresco painting.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:40.699
When he was 14, Michelangelo went one step
too far.

00:05:40.699 --> 00:05:43.619
He had copied some drawings that his master
had done of women.

00:05:43.619 --> 00:05:48.220
But then he dared to ‘correct’ some finer
aspects of the originals by going over them

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.249
with a broad-nibbed pen.

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:51.650
This was too much.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.460
Ghirlandaio kicked Michelangelo out of the
workshop, claiming that there was no teaching

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:56.530
this ‘know-it-all.’

00:05:56.530 --> 00:06:00.319
Rather than being discouraged, the talented
teen made the decision that paint and plaster

00:06:00.319 --> 00:06:01.319
were not for him.

00:06:01.319 --> 00:06:03.939
He considered them to be a lesser form of
art.

00:06:03.939 --> 00:06:08.669
To him the real skill lay in stone, producing
fine sculptors of the human form.

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:13.319
This desire would take him to the very pinnacle
of Florentine nobility the house of Medici.

00:06:13.319 --> 00:06:22.309
The House of Medici

00:06:22.309 --> 00:06:29.020
Before Michelangelo had been dismissed by
Ghirlandaio, some members of the ruling Medici

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:30.770
family visited the workshop.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:34.460
Among them was an elderly sculptor named Bertoldo
di Giovanni.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:38.310
This man noticed the talent that Michelangelo
possessed, and he struck up a relationship

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:39.460
with the boy.

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:43.279
After his dismissal, the budding young sculptor
sought out the older man.

00:06:43.279 --> 00:06:48.999
Bertoldo was employed by Lorenzo de’ Medici,
the most powerful man in the city, to make

00:06:48.999 --> 00:06:51.849
statues and medals and to look after his art
collection.

00:06:51.849 --> 00:06:56.289
Many of Lorenzo’s statues were displayed
in a garden where artist went to draw and

00:06:56.289 --> 00:06:57.289
carve.

00:06:57.289 --> 00:07:01.229
This area was a gathering place for student
sculptors, who would get feedback and instruction

00:07:01.229 --> 00:07:03.789
from Bertoldo about their work.

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:06.800
Soon, Michelangelo was a regular in the garden.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:10.979
The now fifteen year old began creating sculptures
from clay and bringing them to the garden.

00:07:10.979 --> 00:07:15.509
Though he was younger than the other budding
sculptors, his skin was clearly beyond theirs.

00:07:15.509 --> 00:07:16.930
This led to jealousy.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:18.919
Some times this led to physical violence.

00:07:18.919 --> 00:07:21.789
In one encounter, Michelangelo had his nose
broken.

00:07:21.789 --> 00:07:24.009
But the boy was not deterred.

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:28.659
Begging a piece of marble from another sculptor
he created his first relief carving - the

00:07:28.659 --> 00:07:29.659
head of a faun.

00:07:29.659 --> 00:07:33.979
Just as he had been hoping, this sculpture
was seen by Lorenzo Medici, who was immediately

00:07:33.979 --> 00:07:35.499
taken with the boy’s skill.

00:07:35.499 --> 00:07:40.490
He brought Michelangelo into his fold, giving
him his own room and paying him an allowance.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:43.129
The young artist’s life was now more peaceful.

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:46.759
He was able to settle down to the task of
perfecting his chosen artform.

00:07:46.759 --> 00:07:51.209
One of his first major projects was to carve
a scene from mythology on marble as a relief

00:07:51.209 --> 00:07:53.819
panel - the battle of the centaurs.

00:07:53.819 --> 00:07:58.039
These creatures with the heads and arms of
men and bodies of horses gave him plenty of

00:07:58.039 --> 00:08:00.509
scope to show action and form.

00:08:00.509 --> 00:08:05.619
The realism and gentle precision of this work
is remarkable for a sculptor of such a young

00:08:05.619 --> 00:08:06.619
age.

00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:11.279
The other major piece that Michelangelo produced
while working in the House of medici was a

00:08:11.279 --> 00:08:13.809
relief panel called Madonna of the Steps.

00:08:13.809 --> 00:08:16.770
It shows the virgin Mary nursing the infant
Jesus.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:21.491
The piece is carved on fine white marble and
shows the Madonna’s face in profile, as

00:08:21.491 --> 00:08:23.719
she holds her infant son against her.

00:08:23.719 --> 00:08:26.139
Things were going remarkably well for the
17 year old.

00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:30.369
He was living in the inner sanctum of the
richest family in the city and he had a powerful

00:08:30.369 --> 00:08:32.479
patron who admired his talent.

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:34.629
Unfortunately, it wasn’t to last.

00:08:34.629 --> 00:08:37.419
In April, 1492, Lorenzo suddenly died.

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:40.889
His son, Pierro took over, but he cared little
for sculpture.

00:08:40.889 --> 00:08:45.420
He gave, Michelangelo no work until the winter,
when he decided that he would like a snowman

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:46.420
made.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:47.800
He ordered Michangelo to make one.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:51.040
It would prove to be the artist’s strangest
commission.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.120
Around this same time, the prior of Santo
Spirito asked the young sculptor to make a

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:56.920
crucifix for his church.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:01.040
Delighted with the result, the prior give
Michelangelo the use of a room in which he

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.040
could work.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:05.870
Here he was able to dissect corpses in order
to study anatomy.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:14.100
In order to make the most life-like sculptures
of the human body, he believed that he had

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.339
to know how it was put together.

00:09:16.339 --> 00:09:31.610
Thus, he became one of the first people to
dissect and study the human body in this way.

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:32.610
A New Patron

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:34.500
By 1494, Florence was facing a crisis.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:39.180
An army led by King Charles VIII of France
as at the walls threatening to overrun the

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:40.180
city.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:42.550
Many people, including Michelangelo, fled
the city.

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:46.800
He traveled to Bologna in northern Italy,
and then on to Venice, an important trading

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:47.800
city.

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:49.649
Here Michelangelo hoped to find work.

00:09:49.649 --> 00:09:53.420
However, he could not find any employment,
so he went back to Bologna.

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:57.080
Having lost his old patron, Michelangelo set
out in search of a replacement.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:02.000
He found it in the form of Gianfrancesco Aldrovandi,
a local noble and politician.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.980
Aldrovandi set his talented new sculptor to
the task of carving the missing figures at

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:09.630
the unfinished shrine of San Domenico in the
church of the same name.

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:12.709
Again Michelangelo, now twenty, had fallen
on his feet.

00:10:12.709 --> 00:10:17.189
He enjoyed a comfortable home, had stone and
supplies provided and received commissions

00:10:17.189 --> 00:10:18.189
for his artwork.

00:10:18.189 --> 00:10:21.530
Just as had happened in Florence, this bred
resentment among other sculptors.

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:28.569
They paid rent, bought tools and stone, and
survived out of their own pockets.

00:10:28.569 --> 00:10:34.460
Return to Florence

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:38.639
Meanwhile, back in Florence, a change of power
had occurred.

00:10:38.639 --> 00:10:43.000
A priest by the name of Girolamo Savonarola
had instigated a fierce campaign against the

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:44.060
House of Medici.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:48.709
When the threat of French invasion had emerged,
the House of Medici had fled the city, leaving

00:10:48.709 --> 00:10:50.959
Savonarola free to take power.

00:10:50.959 --> 00:10:54.690
This strict churchman disapproved of paintings
and sculptors that showed the naked human

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:55.690
body.

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:58.810
As a result, prospects were not good for local
sculptors.

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:03.689
However, in 1495, Michelangelo felt a great
urge to return to Florence to see his family.

00:11:03.689 --> 00:11:08.149
While there, he made contact with some members
of the House of Medici, now living under false

00:11:08.149 --> 00:11:09.149
names.

00:11:09.149 --> 00:11:10.149
One of them.

00:11:10.149 --> 00:11:14.040
Lorenzo di Pierfrancesco de’ Medici wanted
Michelangelo to help him sell a statue that

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:15.180
he had made earlier.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.790
It was a figure of the Roman god of love,
Cupid.

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:22.040
If the statue was aged to look like an ancient
statue, Lorenzo reasoned, it would make a

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.260
lot of money in Rome.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:27.579
Michelangelo smeared the statue with dirt
to make it look as if it had been buried.

00:11:27.579 --> 00:11:32.740
He then took the forged antique to Rome where
it was purchased by a senior churchman, Cardinal

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:33.740
Riario.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:37.490
While in Rome, Michelangelo was introduced
to some powerful people, including members

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:40.699
of the Borgia family, who numbered among them
the Pope.

00:11:40.699 --> 00:11:45.709
Cardinal Riario, who was well aware that Michelangelo
was really the creator of Cupid, quickly commissioned

00:11:45.709 --> 00:11:48.350
him to create another statue for his collection.

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:53.400
The statue that resulted has come to be regarded
as Michelangelo's first masterpiece - the

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:57.720
statue of Bacchus, the Greek god of wine and
celebrations.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:01.709
Yet, although everyone else was impressed,
the cardinal was not.

00:12:01.709 --> 00:12:05.460
He thought the god looked too drunk and refused
to pay for the work.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:09.350
Michelangelo now had to fight to get paid
for a job he had already completed.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:13.279
On top of this, he received news that his
father had fallen into bad financial straits.

00:12:13.279 --> 00:12:23.160
What he needed, and fast, was a new patron
to support not just himself, but also his

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:28.620
family.

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:29.620
The Pieta

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:32.440
For several months, nothing seemed to pan
out for the 21 year-old master sculptor.

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:36.839
He was without a patron,he was without payment
for his Bacchus statue and his father was

00:12:36.839 --> 00:12:38.759
in desperate need of financial assistance.

00:12:38.759 --> 00:12:42.240
Then, In November of 1497 things took a turn
for the better.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:46.329
He was commissioned to produce an exciting
new work by a French Ambassador by the name

00:12:46.329 --> 00:12:49.089
of Cardinal Jean Bilheres de Lagraulas.

00:12:49.089 --> 00:12:53.910
The Cardinal envisioned a statue of the Virgin
Mary cradling the dead Jesus, a subject known

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:54.910
as the pieta.

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:58.940
It took Michelangelo two years to complete
the Pieta - but it was worth it.

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:02.990
The finished work was displayed in the Vatican
and everyone who saw it was amazed at the

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:07.790
realism of the flesh and fabric that the sculptor
had fashioned from cold marble.

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:09.589
The Pieta made Michelangelo famous.

00:13:09.589 --> 00:13:13.040
Now, the most powerful men in Europe were
clamoring to have him do work for them.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.269
It seemed as if his money woes were well and
truly behind him.

00:13:17.269 --> 00:13:25.949
Artist to the Pope

00:13:25.949 --> 00:13:29.889
Back in Florence, the scene had changed once
again, Savonarola, the priest, had fallen

00:13:29.889 --> 00:13:31.839
into disfavor and been executed.

00:13:31.839 --> 00:13:35.700
It was now safe for artists to return and
pursue projects there.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:39.899
The authorities of the Florentine cathedral
began to formulate the idea to commission

00:13:39.899 --> 00:13:44.370
a giant statue of the Biblical killer of Goliath,
young David.

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:46.459
Two sculptors had already been commissioned
to do the job.

00:13:46.459 --> 00:13:50.920
Both had failed, merely managing to make the
huge block of marble almost unusable.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:52.800
In 1501, the job was given to Michelangelo.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:57.250
In order to do the job, the sculptor had to
stop working on another job in Rome and relocate

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:58.310
to Florence.

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:02.339
This flitting from one project to another
became a common practice, and one that frequently

00:14:02.339 --> 00:14:04.449
created legal ramifications.

00:14:04.449 --> 00:14:08.460
The creation of David was an immensely challenging
and lengthy job.

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:12.420
The first decision was to choose a pose that
fitted in with the way the marble had already

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:13.420
been worked.

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:18.089
This prevented him from emulating the traditional
pose, which showed David standing with Goliath’s

00:14:18.089 --> 00:14:19.569
head at his feet.

00:14:19.569 --> 00:14:24.649
So, he decided to depict David in the moments
before the fight as he were looking upon his

00:14:24.649 --> 00:14:25.649
foe.

00:14:25.649 --> 00:14:28.790
His next move was to surround the huge marble
with barricades.

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:30.230
The he went to work.

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:32.290
He tirelessly chipped away at the giant.

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:35.170
Three years later, the statue of David was
completed.

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:40.120
It was a triumph, with crowds lavishing rich
praise on the creator of the 14-foot nude

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:41.120
figure.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:46.149
The man who had commissioned the work, Piero
Soderini, ruler of Florence, was also impressed

00:14:46.149 --> 00:14:49.309
- but he told Michelangelo that David’s
nose was too broad.

00:14:49.309 --> 00:14:51.430
The sculptor’s stubborn pride now come to
the fore.

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.480
But it was tempered with common sense.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:57.389
Taking a handful of marble dust, he climbed
up the scaffolding and pretended to chisel

00:14:57.389 --> 00:14:58.389
the nose.

00:14:58.389 --> 00:15:00.199
He then let the dust fall to the ground.

00:15:00.199 --> 00:15:04.930
Soderini declared that the nose was now much
better.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:09.720
Sistine Chapel

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:17.140
In 1503, a new pope, Julius II was elected
in Rome.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:22.430
Julius had great artistic ambitions, and he
knew who he wanted to spearhead the many projects

00:15:22.430 --> 00:15:24.170
he had in mind.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:29.769
Only Italy's greatest sculptor, Michelangelo,
had the skills needed to do justice to the

00:15:29.769 --> 00:15:32.869
great theocratic works that the pope envisioned.

00:15:32.869 --> 00:15:37.910
Julius knocked down the unstable fifth-century
Basilica of St. Peter’s in the Vatican and

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.699
began rebuilding the church on a much grander
scale.

00:15:40.699 --> 00:15:45.949
The centerpiece was to be his own tomb, which
would be designed and carved by Michelangelo.

00:15:45.949 --> 00:15:50.629
This massive project was intended to show
the greatness of Julius as both a pope and

00:15:50.629 --> 00:15:51.810
a patron of the arts.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:56.029
It was to be three storeys high and covered
in life-sized sculptures.

00:15:56.029 --> 00:15:59.930
The sculptor spent the first eight months
just quarrying the marble need for the project.

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:04.240
It would prove to be his most frustrating
project, one on which he would work, on and

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:06.040
off, for the next 40 years.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:09.779
In the end the finished tomb was smaller than
planned, but still vast.

00:16:09.779 --> 00:16:14.630
Only three of the statues were by Michelangelo
- the large, bearded Moses, and the Biblical

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:16.620
characters Rachel and Leah.

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:21.500
And, instead of being placed in St. Peter’s,
it was put in the much less grand church of

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:22.879
San Pietro.

00:16:22.879 --> 00:16:27.139
Four years into the tomb project, Pope Julius
set his sights on the ceiling of the Sistine

00:16:27.139 --> 00:16:28.680
Chapel in the Vatican.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:32.579
The ceiling was painted with a pattern of
gold stars but the Pope envisioned something

00:16:32.579 --> 00:16:33.879
far grander.

00:16:33.879 --> 00:16:38.459
Even though Michelangelo was known as a sculptor
and not a painter, the Pope chose him to do

00:16:38.459 --> 00:16:39.459
the work.

00:16:39.459 --> 00:16:43.879
The great sculptor had not worked on a fresco
since his days as an apprentice back in Florence.

00:16:43.879 --> 00:16:46.820
But when the pope chose you to a job, you
did it.

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:50.040
But that didn’t mean that you had to it
willingly.

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.890
Michelangelo considered himself to be a slave
to the whims of the Pope.

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:54.850
He wrote about the painting project . . .

00:16:54.850 --> 00:16:56.220
This is not my profession.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:17:00.490
I am wasting my time, and all for nothing,
may God help me!

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:01.740
It was a massive job.

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:08.579
The chapel's ceiling covered a curved surface
of about 5,600 square feet (507 square meters).

00:17:08.579 --> 00:17:12.929
In Addition, the ceiling had an uneven surface
- and it was 60 feet above the chapel’s

00:17:12.929 --> 00:17:13.929
floor.

00:17:13.929 --> 00:17:18.500
The Pope commissioned the artist to fill this
canvas with scenes from the Old Testament.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:21.520
Michelangelo planned out nine scenes taken
from the book of Genesis.

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:24.350
The centerpiece would be the creation of Adam.

00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:27.649
For four pains-staking years, Michelangelo
worked on the ceiling.

00:17:27.649 --> 00:17:29.210
It was back-breaking work.

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:32.950
The figures were designed to be seen from
the floor, but the artist had to paint high

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:35.140
up on scaffolding, lying on his back.

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:39.850
According to his official biographer, Condivi,
he worked on his own, applying the plaster,

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:42.380
mixing the colors and doing all the painting.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.220
The results were breathtaking.

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:48.399
The use of perspective and the dramatic rendition
of the figures were admired by everyone who

00:17:48.399 --> 00:17:49.410
saw it.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:54.309
Having triumphantly completed the Sistine
Chapel project, Michelangelo returned to his

00:17:54.309 --> 00:17:56.029
work on the tomb of Julius.

00:17:56.029 --> 00:18:00.370
Wen Pope Julius died in 1513, the urgency
went out of the project.

00:18:00.370 --> 00:18:05.780
In 1517, the new Pope, Leo X, asked Michelangelo
to design a new facade for the church of San

00:18:05.780 --> 00:18:07.059
Lorenzo in Florence.

00:18:07.059 --> 00:18:11.029
Though he had no experience as an architect,
the sculptor was confident that he could do

00:18:11.029 --> 00:18:12.039
a good job.

00:18:12.039 --> 00:18:15.760
He threw himself into the project, buying
a piece of land near the church in order to

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:16.760
work on his facade.

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:21.570
But, then, in 1520, the project was put on
hold and then cancelled all together.

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:23.450
Michelangelo was furious.

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:27.789
He had invested himself fully, both physically
and financially into the project.

00:18:27.789 --> 00:18:32.340
Partly to placate him, the Pope gave him a
replacement project - the Medici Tombs, to

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:37.340
be created in remembrance of two members of
the family who had died prematurely.

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:41.820
Michelangelo began work on the Medical Chapel
in 1519, to which was added a library a few

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:42.820
years later.

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:49.460
Both buildings took years to complete and
are stunning designs.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:53.390
Crisis

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:59.809
The artist was now at the height of his creative
powers.

00:18:59.809 --> 00:19:01.970
But crisis was just around the corner.

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:06.120
In 1527, armies from France and the Holy Roman
Empire invaded Italy.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:08.080
Rome was looted and burned.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:12.440
By now there was yet another pope, Clement
VII and he sided with the holy roman Empire

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:13.899
when it besieged Florence.

00:19:13.899 --> 00:19:17.660
A loyal Florentine, Michelangelo returned
home to help defend his city.

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:20.690
He set to work designing new fortifications
to help protect the city.

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:22.799
But his efforts were in vain.

00:19:22.799 --> 00:19:26.670
After a long struggle, the city fell, with
many people being killed.

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:30.559
Michelangelo went into hiding, concealing
himself in a tiny crypt under the altar in

00:19:30.559 --> 00:19:32.860
the Medici Chapel in San Lorenzo.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:39.530
There he stayed until news came through that
the Pope had no animosity against him.

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:47.970
The Waning Years

00:19:47.970 --> 00:19:51.620
Michelangelo spent his latter years writing
poetry and continuing to work for the popes

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:52.620
in Rome.

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:56.360
One of his most famous poems describes how
uncomfortable he was when painting the Sistine

00:19:56.360 --> 00:20:00.419
Chapel ceiling, bent almost double, with paint
splashing on his face.

00:20:00.419 --> 00:20:03.809
As he aged, he wrote more poems of a religious
nature.

00:20:03.809 --> 00:20:08.250
Throughout the reigns of several Popes, he
held the position of chief architect at St.

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:09.250
Peters.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:12.769
Meanwhile he worked on carvings that were
variations of the work that had first propelled

00:20:12.769 --> 00:20:14.419
him to fame, the Pieta.

00:20:14.419 --> 00:20:19.130
It was while working on one of his Pietas
on February 12th, 1564 that he began to feel

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:20.130
feverish.

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:24.000
He took to his bed, and despite the efforts
of his doctors, died six days later.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:40.640
His dying wish was to have his body be returned
to Florence - the city 

00:20:40.640 --> 00:21:00.429
that he loved.

